---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../components/Breadcrumbs.astro';
---

<Layout
  title="Fact-Checking Process | devsyllabus"
  description="How devsyllabus fact-checks technical content: primary sources, reviewer verification, and what happens when a claim cannot be confirmed."
  path="/fact-checking/"
>
  <Breadcrumbs crumbs={[{ label: 'Editorial Policy', href: '/editorial-policy/' }, { label: 'Fact-Checking' }]} />

  <div class="doc">
  <h1>Fact-checking process</h1>

  <h2>Source hierarchy</h2>
  <p>Claims are verified against sources in this order of authority:</p>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Clinical guidelines and standard references</strong> — WHO guidance, national treatment guidelines, and standard laboratory reference ranges.</li>
    <li><strong>Canonical texts</strong> — the standard textbook for the subject (Gray's, Guyton &amp; Hall, Robbins &amp; Cotran, Katzung, Park's).</li>
    <li><strong>Primary literature</strong> — peer-reviewed papers and systematic reviews for claims that go beyond textbook teaching.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    Blog posts and third-party tutorials inform our writing but are not
    cited as authority for technical claims.
  </p>

  <h2>What gets checked</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Every definition in a note's key terms section.</li>
    <li>Every code example, including expected outputs.</li>
    <li>Every practice question's answer and explanation.</li>
    <li>Dates, version numbers, and named standards.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>When sources disagree</h2>
  <p>
    Different textbooks teach some topics differently, particularly
    normal-form edge cases and language-convention questions. When
    authoritative sources disagree, the note presents the mainstream
    position and flags the disagreement, so exam answers can be adapted
    to the course's convention.
  </p>

  <h2>Unverifiable claims</h2>
  <p>
    A claim we cannot verify against a source in the hierarchy above does
    not get published. See also the
    <a href="/editorial-policy/">editorial policy</a> and
    <a href="/corrections/">corrections policy</a>.
  </p>
  </div>
</Layout>
